Pending packaging follow-ups
============================

Repack for +dfsg (not urgent)
-----------------------------

Only real DFSG violations need removing; everything else (Windows-only C++
modules, PowerShell, .psd/.vsdx sources, templates, .NET helpers) is free and
unused by the agent-only build, so it can stay in the tarball.

Must remove (DFSG #2 - prebuilt binary, no source):
  installers/installer-NSCP/wixca.dll      PE32 DLL (WiX 2.0.5325.0, CPL-1.0)
  libs/protobuf_net/Google.Protobuf.dll    PE32 .NET assembly (BSD-3-Clause)

The licenses themselves are fine (confirmed by upstream); the violation is
only the missing source form in the tarball.

Review (borderline, not installed, safe to drop):
  docs/source/realtime-monitoring.vsdx     Visio file, never installed

Log to the journal instead of a file (evaluate)
-----------------------------------------------

The unit runs the agent with --log-backend threaded-file, so it writes
/var/log/nsclient/nsclient.log and debian/nsclient.logrotate rotates it with
copytruncate. Logging to the journal instead - by passing --log-backend console,
since a service with no flag now picks threaded-file itself - would drop the
rotation snippet, the LogsDirectory= and the copytruncate window in which lines
written during the copy are lost (nscp does not reopen its log on a signal), and
would give the usual journalctl -u filtering and retention.

Two things to check before switching:
  - the console backend writes its own timestamp and tags no priority, so
    journalctl -p could not filter by level until upstream emits a prefix
    systemd understands;
  - errors go to stderr and everything else to stdout, which the journal keeps
    apart but an admin used to tailing one file may not expect.

check_nscp is unaffected either way: its error count is kept in memory, it does
not read the log file.
